    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm running a small organic kale operation out west and i've noticed some yellowing in the lower leaves. soil test shows my ph is at 6.2 and i just put down some compost. i need to boost nitrogen but i dont want to mess up the ph too much. should i go with bone meal or blood meal for the N levels?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "At a pH of 6.2, neither bone meal nor blood meal is the ideal nitrogen source since blood meal can significantly raise soil pH and may temporarily inhibit nutrient availability in calcium-sulfur-based soils. For an organic kale operation in the west, it would be better to opt for composted manure or whey powder, which provide nitrogen without drastically altering pH and align well with your recent compost application. Additionally, ensuring your potash levels are adequate is crucial for kale's overall development.",
