      "content": "Blood meal inevitably raises soil pH when applied at the rates typically needed for kale, so stabilizing the nutrient uptake without fixing this is not recommended. While bulk application might lower costs, the resulting alkalinity could eventually lock up your other essential minerals until soil calcium sorption sites are full. It might be more effective to spread a slim amount of blood meal heavily mixed with compost or compost tea to minimize localized pH spikes when soil test phosphorus levels are also considered.",
